   211	
   212	        <h3>Ledger and query interfaces stay separate</h3>
   213	        <p>
   214	          <code>ArrivalLedger</code> owns custody operations. <code>ArrivalQuery</code> owns facts,
   215	          ticks, folds, search, and SQL-oriented reads. A query handle MUST NOT expose a generic
   250	          A backend with an atomic limit above one can append several ledger records in one
   251	          transaction for import efficiency. A backend whose safe limit is one remains
   252	          conforming and imports by verified single-record steps. A
   253	          <code>batch</code> record is different: it expresses one application ceremony whose
   254	          multiple fact rows share one ordinal. Backend batching must not rewrite one semantic
   255	          batch into several fact records or vice versa.
   256	        </p>
   257	
   258	        <h3>Network retries</h3>
   259	        <p>
   260	          A remote Authority SHOULD support an opaque idempotency key scoped to the lineage and
   261	          authenticated caller. Repeating the same key and request returns the original commit;
   262	          reusing it with different content refuses. The mapping is backend metadata, not a
   263	          ledger field. Without that capability, a lost response produces an “outcome unknown”
   264	          result and the caller reconciles by reading the expected successor and its authored
   265	          content commitment before deciding whether to retry.
   266	        </p>
   267	
   268	        <h3>Typed refusals</h3>
   269	        <p>
   270	          The failure conditions this contract names — head mismatch, same-height fork, unknown
   271	          backend, atomic limit exceeded, and not-authority — surface as typed refusals under one
   272	          backend-neutral root, so a caller catches a refusal without knowing which adapter
   273	          produced it. A capability a backend deliberately does not offer is typed
   274	          <code>NotSupported</code> under that same root: an absence <code>capabilities()</code>
   275	          already reports is a contract answer, not a fault. Every other failure is
   276	          backend-specific BY DESIGN. The root covers exactly what the contract text asserts and
   277	          no more, because an adapter that lumped its own faults into these would be making a
   278	          claim the contract cannot back
   279	          (<code>decision:design/arrival-slice2-contract-text</code>, ruling 2).
   280	        </p>
   281	      </section>
   282	
   283	      <section class="doc-section" id="durability">
   284	        <div class="doc-section-header">
   285	          <span class="doc-section-number">05</span>
   286	          <h2>Durability and failure semantics</h2>
   287	        </div>
   288	        <p>
   289	          “Durable” is meaningless without a failure boundary. Every Authority reports a named
   290	          profile and its limits; callers may require a stronger profile than a backend offers.
   395	          <table class="doc-spec-table">
   396	            <thead>
   397	              <tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Storage cost</th><th>Freshness</th><th>Recovery</th></tr>
   398	            </thead>
   399	            <tbody>
   400	              <tr><td>Direct record query</td><td>No duplicate projection rows</td><td>At captured head</td><td>No rebuild; query logic must understand bodies</td></tr>
   401	              <tr><td>Views / generated columns</td><td>Mostly indexes</td><td>At captured head</td><td>Recreate schema objects</td></tr>
   402	              <tr><td>Transactional materialization</td><td>Selected duplicated fields</td><td>At commit</td><td>Rebuild deterministically from ledger</td></tr>
   403	              <tr><td>Asynchronous projection</td><td>Separate query state</td><td>Explicit watermark</td><td>Resume from verified projected head</td></tr>
   404	            </tbody>
   405	          </table>
   406	        </div>
   407	
   408	        <h3>Projection obligations</h3>
   409	        <ul>
   410	          <li>Every materialized row records or can derive <code>(arrival_ordinal, arrival_seq)</code>.</li>
   411	          <li>Batch expansion order is deterministic and shared by live indexing and full rebuild.</li>
   412	          <li>The projection stores a verified <code>projected_through</code> head, not merely a row count.</li>
   413	          <li>Rows beyond, missing before, or inconsistent with that watermark cause refusal or rebuild.</li>
   414	          <li>Projection state is never used to “repair” canonical ledger history.</li>
   415	          <li>Query results report the lineage and projected prefix they actually represent.</li>
   416	        </ul>
   417	        <p>
   418	          A verified <code>projected_through</code> head is a claim the query surface cannot make
   419	          alone: a projection knows the coordinate it reached, and only custody holds the record
   420	          whose hash completes it. <code>head_at(watermark)</code> in §03 is that resolution — the
   421	          ledger-side read a projection calls to turn its watermark into a verified head, so
   422	          meeting this obligation never requires giving a query handle a route into custody
   423	          (<code>decision:design/arrival-slice2-contract-text</code>, ruling 3).
   424	        </p>
   425	
   426	        <div class="doc-callout">
   427	          <span class="doc-callout-label">DuckDB + Quack shape</span>
   428	          <p>
   429	            The personal backend can keep one canonical <code>arrival_records</code> relation,
   430	            expose Quack-friendly read views, and add only worthwhile indexes or materialized
   431	            columns. No companion <code>.arrival</code> file is required. Quack-facing access is
   432	            read-only with respect to ledger custody; writes use the admission API.
   433	          </p>
   434	        </div>
   435	      </section>
   436	
   437	      <section class="doc-section" id="transfer">
   438	        <div class="doc-section-header">
   439	          <span class="doc-section-number">08</span>
   440	          <h2>Replication, import, and export</h2>
   441	        </div>
   442	
   443	        <h3>Exact replication</h3>
   444	        <p>
   445	          A replica consumes pre-coordinated records. It verifies each record and atomically
   446	          compares its current full head before inserting the exact suffix. It never assigns a
   447	          new lineage, ordinal, predecessor, signature, or record hash. A conflicting same-height
   448	          record is a fork and stops replication.
   449	        </p>
   450	
   451	        <h3>Portable import</h3>
   452	        <p>
   453	          Import targets a new empty replica or staged authority by default. Importing into a
   454	          non-empty target requires exact prefix agreement through the target head. The importer
   455	          then appends only the verified remainder. “Merge” that re-coordinates content is an
   456	          admission operation into another lineage, not replication. Import is a procedure over
   457	          the operations §03 names, not an operation of its own: the table gives it no row, and
   458	          one backend’s import stays a convention until a second backend shows which of it is
   459	          shared (<code>decision:design/arrival-slice2-contract-text</code>, ruling 4).
   460	        </p>
   461	
   462	        <div class="doc-callout is-open">
   463	          <span class="doc-callout-label">Contract debt — resumable import</span>
   464	          <p>
   465	            Resumable import is required for conforming limited backends; the reference file
   466	            backend does not yet implement it. A backend whose atomic limit is below the verified
   467	            remainder cannot land that remainder in one step, so a single-shot importer refuses
   468	            forever instead of progressing — §04 already says such a backend imports by verified
   469	            single-record steps, and §12 exercises import among the configured limits. The
   470	            importer that advances in bounded, resumable steps is therefore contract-implied, and
   471	            it is a design rather than a fix: build it when a limited backend exists
   472	            (<code>decision:design/arrival-slice2-contract-text</code>, ruling 5).
   473	          </p>
   474	        </div>
   475	
   476	        <h3>Canonical export</h3>
   477	        <p>
   478	          Export captures one head, scans its complete prefix in ordinal order, re-encodes each
   479	          record with the selected wire codec, and writes a manifest. An implementation that
   480	          stores typed columns MUST prove that the reconstructed logical JSON values reproduce
   180	index cannot answer the only question a writer may ask.
   181	
   182	`store "….arrival"` → `ArrivalStore` over the sibling `….db`; `store
   183	"….jsonl"` → `JsonlStore` over the sibling `….db`; `store "….db"` → plain
   184	`SqliteStore`. Never construct a store for a vertex by hand — go through
   185	`open_canonical_store`, or a direct sqlite write becomes an out-of-band
   186	insert the log doesn't account for.
   187	
   188	**The backend contract is a second, ADDITIVE path — nothing routes through it
   189	yet.** `engine/arrival_contract.py` states
   190	`docs/architecture/arrival/backend-contract.html` §03 in Python: the `Head` /
   191	`Watermark` / `Commit` types, the `ArrivalLedger` (ten ops) and `ArrivalQuery`
   192	Protocols, and the `ContractRefusal` root each adapter translates its own
   193	faults into one-for-one. Stdlib and typing only — no sqlite3, no
   194	`engine.arrival` — so naming a `Head` costs nothing.
   195	`engine/arrival_file_backend.py` is the first adapter (`FileLedger` /
